      <div class="ccarp-week-class">
        <h3>What we did in class</h3>
        <p>Informal introduction; discussed phase structure and weekly rhythm. We also went over many references:</p>
        <ul class="ccarp-refs">
          <li>Using home made knitted cloth satellites, Afroditi Psarra &amp; Audrey Briot intercepted NOAA weather satellites’ audiovisual transmissions using Software-Defined-Radio. <a href="https://afroditipsarra.com/work/listening-space">Listening Space</a></li>
          <li>Circle of Moment measurement by Taeyoon Choi and Enroon Kang, setting your own personal minute. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=anycci3nKhY">Video</a></li>
          <li>The work of <a href="https://lav.io/">Sam Lavigne</a>, who uses the tools of oppressors back on them. One of my favs I didn’t get to show you: <a href="https://lav.io/projects/cold-call/">Cold Call</a>, where you waste the time of the most polluting companies in the world to earn carbon offset credits.</li>
          <li>Buoyant Choreographies: Harmonies of Light, Sound, and Human Connection by Dennis Hong and Yusuke Tanaka. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=C_wZvC2EpYs">Video</a></li>
          <li>The poet engineer <a href="https://www.instagram.com/the.poet.engineer/">Kat Zheng</a>, who has an immaculate sketching practice.</li>
          <li>From 11 April 1980 to 11 April 1981, Tehching Hsieh <a href="https://www.frieze.com/article/tehching-hsieh-durational-performance-235">punched into a time clock installed in his downtown New York studio every hour</a>.</li>
        </ul>
